<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jorge Luis Borges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jorge Luis Borges was an Argentine writer whose short-story collections Ficciones (1944) and The Aleph (1949), with their labyrinths, mirrors and imaginary books, made him one of the most influential authors of the twentieth century. He was also a poet and essayist, and served as director of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s National Library from 1955 to 1973.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jorge-luis-borges.jpg" alt="Jorge Luis Borges" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grete Stern · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jorge_Luis_Borges_1951,_by_Grete_Stern_(full).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Borges was born on 24 August 1899 into an educated middle-class family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, then a poor area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. His mother, Leonor Acevedo Suárez, worked as a translator and came from a family of criollo origin that had been much involved in the Argentine War of Independence. In 1914 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he studied at the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Genève</a>, and travelled widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. When they returned to Buenos Aires in 1921 he had little formal education and few friends, and he brought with him the doctrine of Ultraism. He began publishing poems and essays in literary journals and worked as a librarian and public lecturer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Borges's father died in 1938. On Christmas Eve that year Borges suffered a severe head injury and nearly died of sepsis. While recovering he began exploring the new style for which he became known. His first story after the accident, Pierre Menard, Author of the Quixote, appeared in May 1939, and his first collection of stories, The Garden of Forking Paths, followed in 1941, composed mostly of works already published in Sur.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a>, he was told he was being promoted from his post at the Miguel Cané Library to inspector of poultry and rabbits at a municipal market; asked why, he was told that he had been on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. He resigned the next day, and the Argentine Society of Writers held a dinner in his honour. In 1955 he was appointed director of the National Public Library and professor of English literature at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. He was completely blind by the age of 55. In 1961 he shared the first International Formentor Prize with Samuel Beckett, and by the 1960s his work was widely translated. He was never awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known collections, Ficciones and El Aleph, appeared in the 1940s and explore dreams, labyrinths, chance, infinity, archives, mirrors, fictional writers and mythology. He never wrote a novel; he preferred the short story, finding that a novel required padding. He was also a notable translator from English, French, German, Old English and Old Norse, beginning at ten with a Spanish version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a>'s The Happy Prince. In his view a translation may improve upon the original, and that different, even contradictory, renderings can be equally valid. Borges also practised literary forgery and the review of an imaginary work, a form he drew from Thomas Carlyle's Sartor Resartus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jorge Luis Borges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The country has produced Nobel laureates in science, including Bernardo Houssay, Luis Federico Leloir and César Milstein, writers such as <b>Jorge Luis Borges</b> and Julio Cortázar, and the tango, inscribed by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage in 2009.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The writer <b>Jorge Luis Borges</b> told the Argentine Society of Writers, after Perón became president in 1946, that “dictatorships breed oppression, dictatorships breed servility, dictatorships breed cruelty”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>For T. S. Eliot, “Dante and Shakespeare divide the world between them”; for <b>Jorge Luis Borges</b> it was “the best book literature has achieved”.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The book had a notable impact on the 1960s counterculture and on figures such as John Cage, <b>Jorge Luis Borges</b> and Hermann Hesse.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as Henry Fielding and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are <b>Jorge Luis Borges</b>, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, Victor Hugo, Marcel Proust, Alexander Pushkin, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and W. B. Yeats.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jorge Luis Borges</b> called it a psychological novel written “with an almost miraculous naturalness”, and Yasunari Kawabata, in his Nobel Prize acceptance speech, called it “the highest pinnacle of Japanese literature”.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.6,-58.38&amp;year=1955" aria-label="Buenos Aires on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3067 3416 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jorge Luis Borges"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3517 3686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.6,-58.38&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
